isabeanfuzz on Twitter.

00:21:32.627 --> 00:21:36.327
"Do you believe in the existence
of a quarter life crisis?

00:21:36.327 --> 00:21:40.127
"I'm 37, so obviously closer
to having a midlife crisis,

00:21:40.127 --> 00:21:41.973
"but since in quarantine,

00:21:41.973 --> 00:21:43.637
""I've been thinking it might be possible

00:21:43.637 --> 00:21:46.817
"to take the quarantine
quarter life crisis intensive

00:21:46.817 --> 00:21:48.750
"and get it done before 40."

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:51.810
Yeah, if you're 37 and you're
having a quarter life crisis

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:55.047
that means that you
expect to be 148 years old

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:01.230
if I'm doing the math right.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:06.230
But she's saying, "I'm closer
to having a midlife crisis."

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:09.390
I don't quarter-life crisis, I guess

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:11.340
would be like in your mid 20s.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:13.970
But I do know,
I don't know about that.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.053
I can relate to the fact that, like,

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:18.580
I mean, being in quarantine,

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:23.350
it's time for a lot of reflection.

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.380
And so I've definitely
found myself asking,

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:28.023
what is my life?

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:30.073
What am I doing?

00:22:33.085 --> 00:22:35.199
I wouldn't call it a crisis.

00:22:35.199 --> 00:22:37.680
So, de-emphasizing the word crisis,

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:42.293
I do think I've had like a
midlife assessment period.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:45.600
There's a lot of question
marks associated with that,

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:50.600
but it's, turning 42 and having
time to think about it more,

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:55.983
I just feel like, okay,

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:00.360
what's the rest of my
life gonna look like?

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:02.620
I'm looking back at everything we've done.

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:04.850
And by the way, we've...

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.130
And if you listen through
a bunch of these episodes,

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:09.030
we've lived a bunch of life, man.

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.044
I'm super grateful.

00:23:11.044 --> 00:23:16.044
Like, we've had great lives, multiple.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:17.315
We've had multiple careers.

00:23:17.315 --> 00:23:18.148
You've got multiple?

00:23:18.148 --> 00:23:20.680
I feel like we've lived multiple lives.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:23.110
We've had different careers.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:25.360
And even in this current career,

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.870
there's been like
multiple iterations to it.

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:33.270
And this year, even before the pandemic,

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:37.950
it was kind of a transition
year for us professionally

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.200
because we had come off a touring.

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.467
We'd been ambitious
about a number of things.

00:23:41.467 --> 00:23:44.300
And it was kind of like a regrouping phase

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:48.180
in order to then figure out
where we were going next

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:50.263
and the things we were gonna create next,

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:53.820
in addition to this show
and "Good Mythical Morning".

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:58.100
In terms of building the company,

00:23:58.100 --> 00:23:59.663
on a lot of fronts.

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:04.817
I felt like I'm in this midlife mindset

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.603
even before the quarantine.

00:24:09.934 --> 00:24:12.780
I don't know, can you relate to that?

00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:15.960
Do you feel like you're
having a midlife crisis?

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:19.650
I think that the components
of a midlife crisis

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:21.180
and that's why you're probably feeling it

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:22.363
and I'm feeling it too,

00:24:23.614 --> 00:24:28.614
you've been working towards something

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:30.630
and it doesn't have to
necessarily be your career

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:34.303
could just be like you just trying to get,

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:36.400
you're trying to do life.

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:37.843
And there's a certain part of your life

00:24:37.843 --> 00:24:42.310
when you're really trying
hard to do life and to focus

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:45.110
and to move things to the next level.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:49.530
And then you start crossing
over into this place

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:51.810
where you sorta realize that,

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:56.070
oh, what I was trying to accomplish

00:24:56.070 --> 00:25:00.720
and kinda set up in my
life, it's kind of happened.

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:04.283
I have a house, I have a car, I have kids,

00:25:05.230 --> 00:25:07.470
I have a relatively stable job.

00:25:07.470 --> 00:25:11.040
And then you start
looking over the horizon

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:15.469
and you're like, "Oh, so it's just this.

00:25:15.469 --> 00:25:17.477
"It's just this and it's just more of this

00:25:17.477 --> 00:25:20.010
"and then one day I die."

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:22.720
And I think that, that
transitional period,

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:24.140
like there's a point in your life where,

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.420
when you're in college or whatever,

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:26.840
all you're thinking about
is what's ahead of you.

00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:27.930
I gotta figure this out,

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:30.400
I gotta make the right decision
about what I'm gonna do.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.780
And then you make...

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:33.761
Like for us,

00:25:33.761 --> 00:25:37.130
we went through the different transitions

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:38.650
to get to the point where
we're working together

00:25:38.650 --> 00:25:40.840
doing this stuff, and then
constantly trying to just

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:43.910
grind and figure it out
and build this business.

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:45.600
Yeah, there was so much scrambling

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:48.490
and yeah, for us, I'm so grateful

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:49.890
that we're at a place where,

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:55.660
we're enjoying stability,
even in the face of

00:25:56.630 --> 00:26:01.060
what's going on economically,
relative stability for us,

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:05.420
not total civility, I'm
still wigging out a lot.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:09.203
But the ability to have a
midlife crisis is a privilege.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:13.027
It means that you've
gotten to a point where

00:26:13.027 --> 00:26:15.800
you're actually able to stop,

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:18.440
step outside of your circumstances

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:21.020
and be like,
Is this all there is?

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.100
Is this it?
Yeah.

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:24.900
I talked about this book a long time ago.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:26.767
You forced the recommendation out of me,

00:26:26.767 --> 00:26:28.387
"The Second Mountain",

00:26:29.505 --> 00:26:30.990
where it talks about,

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:32.670
you work really hard to
get to the first mountain.

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:35.140
And, you either fall
off the first mountain

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:37.310
or you get to the top
of it and at that point,

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:39.540
you realize, this can't be my mountain.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.760
And I think that one of the
things that's coinciding

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:46.760
with our midlife crisis, if
that's, I will use that word.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:50.970
You can use assessment,
I'll say crisis is,

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:53.910
I think there's no mystery
in why this is the year

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:57.680
that we decided to tell our
full story about the last years.

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:01.810
There's no mystery why I'm willing to say

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:04.920
the things that I say on Twitter.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:09.563
And it's because I think that this life

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:13.440
has got to be bigger than me

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:17.380
just trying to be a successful person

00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:20.023
and make it on YouTube or
whatever it is that we,

00:27:20.023 --> 00:27:21.520
what we call what we're trying to do.

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:22.727
So I was like, "Oh, no,

00:27:22.727 --> 00:27:25.697
"actually, I kinda need to think about

00:27:25.697 --> 00:27:28.080
"the mark that I'm leaving."
Legacy.

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:32.250
Yeah and it's, I think that
you just naturally become...

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:35.150
So it's all about , everyone
is gonna have a crisis.

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:37.023
The question is, what
is your response to it?

00:27:37.023 --> 00:27:38.830
Are you gonna buy a Ferrari?

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:40.380
Well, I'm not--
Are you just gonna become-

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:42.490
You know what my Ferrari is gonna be,

00:27:42.490 --> 00:27:43.500
because I've been thinking,

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:45.190
I've been talking to you about this.

00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:48.163
I've started looking at #vanlife.

00:27:49.264 --> 00:27:52.680
I don't know what planted
this seed in my mind,

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:56.810
but I'm like, people are
modifying these vans,

00:27:56.810 --> 00:27:58.190
like the sprinter vans.

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:01.440
And, once you click on
one of those on Instagram,

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:03.260
Instagram knows you wanna look

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:04.880
at the inside of people's vans.

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:08.410
So I'm like, man, these people
are like traveling around

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:11.650
from state to state,
sleeping and living in a van.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:14.530
They're parking their van
next to a mountain lake,

00:28:14.530 --> 00:28:17.040
opening the back doors of the van

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:21.590
and taking a picture
from their bed to a lake.

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:22.970
I saw this one clip.

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:25.600
I appreciate this.
Somebody had a,

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.980
they had like a GoPro
stuck to their forehead

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:31.776
and they got out of
their bed in their van,

00:28:31.776 --> 00:28:35.660
they scooped up a coffee
mug, poured coffee into it,

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:37.610
opened the door to the van

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:39.450
and it was a beautiful mountain lake.

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:44.410
And then they walk out onto
this little stone pathway.

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:47.680
And then they just, with their coffee,

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:50.060
walk on the pathway and
just jump into the lake.

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:53.510
And I was like, that's gonna
be the rest of my life.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:56.426
I'mma get one of these vans.

00:28:56.426 --> 00:28:58.420
And I'm talking to you about it.

00:28:58.420 --> 00:29:00.270
I'm talking to my kids about it.

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:02.137
Lando is into it, Christie's like,

00:29:02.137 --> 00:29:03.950
"Where's the shower in this thing?"

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:04.827
I'm like,

00:29:04.827 --> 00:29:07.959
"I think you have to shower
outside unless you think..."

00:29:07.959 --> 00:29:10.130
I don't wanna be, well, first of all,

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:12.130
I was talking to, I told you about this.

00:29:13.430 --> 00:29:16.000
I was talking to my friend's brother

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:18.630
who had a van that they were redoing

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:20.500
and he was creating a system

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:22.170
that recycled three gallons of water

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:23.570
so you could take a shower inside the van.

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:24.640
He was like trying to...

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:25.473
Yeah, that's right.

00:29:25.473 --> 00:29:27.110
But he's just a dude.

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:28.940
And it's him and his wife.

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:31.610
I think you planted the
seed when you told me that.

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:34.603
Well, but I really, I believe in this,

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:38.110
but I have one hangup for me personally

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:39.430
and one hangup for you personally,

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:41.790
that I don't think no as
a showstopper necessarily.

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:45.240
You know me, I'm just
a big man and #vanlife

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:48.630
probably doesn't have a lot of
six foot, seven aficionados.

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:51.403
As long as your knees
bend, you'll be all right.

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:54.790
But the second thing
is, is that you have...

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:56.710
It's a family of five.

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:59.110
So, is this when the kids are gone?

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:02.303
I'm thinking about like
making a cut to the team,

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:05.360
to make this van life thing happen.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:07.597
It's like, well, "are you in or not?

00:30:07.597 --> 00:30:09.497
"'Cause everyone's not gonna make it."

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:15.080
The idea of doing van life with my family

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:17.390
is not something that I'm into.

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:20.770
I'll do a vacation with
them, I'll stay in a hotel.

00:30:20.770 --> 00:30:22.453
I'm talking about an RV.

00:30:22.453 --> 00:30:24.920
I'm not talking about living in a van.

00:30:24.920 --> 00:30:27.720
I'm talking about having the ultra luxury

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:31.190
of just being able to get
into the van every other month

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:32.106
and go on like a, and it's like--

00:30:32.106 --> 00:30:34.700
You want me to go with you?

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:35.533
Sometimes.

00:30:35.533 --> 00:30:37.530
Yeah, but isn't it just one bed.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:39.037
I'm not sharing a bed with you.

00:30:39.037 --> 00:30:41.110
You are the worst person in the world

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:43.113
to share a bed with and I
haven't shared a bed with you.

00:30:43.113 --> 00:30:46.900
I don't know, I'm putting
another bed on top.

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:49.220
You can sleep up there in a popup tent.

00:30:49.220 --> 00:30:51.810
I need it just for the sake
of the fan fiction people,

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:53.920
I do need to finish that sentence though.

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:58.690
We used to go to conferences
when we were in college

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:01.920
and after college where forced

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:04.560
college students would
stay in one hotel room

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:09.470
and me and you would share like
a queen sized bed in a hotel

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:14.420
and you are not a good bed partner

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:15.333
because you move a whole lot.

00:31:15.333 --> 00:31:16.560
I said it before, I'll say it again.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:18.710
I sleep with reckless abandoned.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:22.510
You wake up and your face
is right next to my face.

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:24.350
So I definitely do not
wanna be a part of that

00:31:24.350 --> 00:31:26.290
if that's part of #vanlife.

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:27.990
You wait until you see this van.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:32.300
Next question, Ruth asks,

00:31:32.300 --> 00:31:34.517
no, that's RuthDahl 11.

00:31:34.517 --> 00:31:38.367
"Why does time seem to move
exponentially faster each year?"

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:43.950
So, like when you're a kid,
everything goes so slowly.

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:44.830
When you're a teenager,

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:48.130
when you're like the more
you want time to progress,

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:49.140
the less it does.

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:51.330
And then the more you
want it to slow down,

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:52.852
the more it speeds up.

00:31:52.852 --> 00:31:54.850
I guess that rings true for me.

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:56.230
It definitely rings true for me.

00:31:56.230 --> 00:31:58.092
I think it rings true for a lot of people.

00:31:58.092 --> 00:32:02.370
And so I looked up, "is there
any science behind this?"

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:05.183
This is what an article in
scientific American said.

00:32:06.427 --> 00:32:09.087
"Our brain encodes new experiences,

00:32:09.087 --> 00:32:11.717
"but not familiar ones into memory.

00:32:11.717 --> 00:32:13.787
"And our retrospective judgment of time

00:32:13.787 --> 00:32:16.297
"is based on how many
new memories we create

00:32:16.297 --> 00:32:17.467
"over a certain period."

00:32:17.467 --> 00:32:18.547
"In other words,

00:32:18.547 --> 00:32:21.767
"the more new memories we
build on a weekend getaway,

00:32:21.767 --> 00:32:23.857
"the longer that trip
will seem in hindsight.

00:32:23.857 --> 00:32:27.190
"This phenomenon, which Hammond,"

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:31.887
Hammond is, I guess the
doctor that they're quoting.

00:32:31.887 --> 00:32:35.247
"has dubbed "the holiday
paradox" seems to present

00:32:35.247 --> 00:32:37.517
"one of the best clues
as to why in retrospect,

00:32:37.517 --> 00:32:39.657
"time seems to pass more
quickly older we get."

00:32:39.657 --> 00:32:41.747
"From childhood to early adulthood,

00:32:41.747 --> 00:32:43.507
"we have many fresh experiences

00:32:43.507 --> 00:32:45.037
"and learn countless new skills.

00:32:45.037 --> 00:32:48.217
"As adults though, our
lives become more routine

00:32:48.217 --> 00:32:50.710
"and we experience fewer
unfamiliar events."

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:53.217
Just like I said, is this all there is?

00:32:53.217 --> 00:32:55.207
"As a result, our early years

00:32:55.207 --> 00:32:57.067
"tend to be relatively overrepresented

00:32:57.067 --> 00:32:59.927
"in our autobiographical
memory and on reflection

00:32:59.927 --> 00:33:02.107
"seem to have lasted longer."

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:07.020
So it's not that in the moment...

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.510
If you sit an old person
down and a young person down

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:10.623
for an hour,

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:13.310
it's actually the probably
the younger person

00:33:13.310 --> 00:33:15.670
who's gonna feel like that
hour is taking longer.

00:33:15.670 --> 00:33:17.550
Oh, I'd actually, that's
consistent with it,

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:19.230
but it's not about that.

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:22.210
It's really just the
fact that you don't have

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:23.230
any points of reference.

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:24.520
You gotta have points of reference

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:26.370
to create memories.
New experiences.

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:28.610
To me, that's the thing,

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:30.470
and I think I'm only this van life thing

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:35.160
because I'm so cooped up
and I wanna go to parks now.

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:36.810
I wanna get out of the house again

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:39.933
and I want to experience adventure and

00:33:43.358 --> 00:33:46.240
maybe it's overblown
because I'm so cooped up

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:50.380
and it'll dissipate over
the rest of the year

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:52.010
as things continue to loosen up

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:54.670
but fingers crossed, I don't know,

00:33:54.670 --> 00:33:57.310
I'm not really making predictions here,

00:33:57.310 --> 00:34:00.140
but I do like the idea of,

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:01.780
and we've talked about this in the past,

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:06.030
like creating experiences, new experiences

00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:08.010
that enrich your life and say, you know,

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.570
I'm a creature of habit, I like to do,

00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:14.850
I like getting things honed and perfected

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:16.793
and then replicated.

00:34:17.850 --> 00:34:19.563
But it's not the,

00:34:21.614 --> 00:34:25.260
that doesn't make for a,

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:26.970
for the best life necessarily.

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:27.960
It actually, I mean,

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:31.040
and according to this, the more routine,

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:32.903
the faster your life is gonna go.

00:34:34.140 --> 00:34:35.940
The faster you're gonna piss it away

00:34:37.140 --> 00:34:38.920
if you don't break out the routine.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:40.750
Not even remember any of it.

00:34:40.750 --> 00:34:41.880
Well, it's funny because

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:43.730
I'm not a routine guy

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:46.020
and I'm actually noticing

00:34:47.619 --> 00:34:49.990
one of the things I've done consistently,

00:34:49.990 --> 00:34:52.260
because we haven't had to
go into the office every day

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:54.050
and so we're not getting,
I'm getting up early,

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:55.370
but I'm not having to get on the road

00:34:55.370 --> 00:34:57.100
at the same time every day.

00:34:57.100 --> 00:34:59.613
Is that, I'm meditating
and I'm working out.

00:35:00.950 --> 00:35:02.970
And then I'm actually
able to walk probably

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:04.620
with Jesse at the end of the day,

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:10.140
but it's getting tougher
for me because it feels

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:15.140
like I go and I like get up
stretch, meditate, workout.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:18.550
Yeah, it's the same old thing.

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:19.410
Do my smoothie.

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:21.620
And then I'm just like, "I
gotta eat something different."

00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:23.107
It takes me about three
weeks and I'm like,

00:35:23.107 --> 00:35:25.720
"I've had this smoothie for three weeks."

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:27.860
and it's like, I start getting stir crazy.

00:35:27.860 --> 00:35:29.578
I'm like, "I gotta have oatmeal.

00:35:29.578 --> 00:35:33.000
"I gotta do something else to mix it up."

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:36.410
But the thing you're talking
about, this cooped up thing.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:38.550
So I think, I don't know
if I made this a wreck.

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:39.510
I should've made it a wreck,

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:41.290
but, did I talk about
the book "Underland"?

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:42.423
Did I talk?
Yeah, yeah.

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:47.760
So when I, at the beginning of quarantine,

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:49.310
I was doing this exercise

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:51.730
where I was sort of figuring
out what my values are.

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:53.870
And I don't mean that in like
a family values kind of way.

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:54.703
I was just like,

00:35:56.572 --> 00:35:58.500
what do I want my life to
be kinda represented by?

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:01.060
What I want to be present in my life.

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:03.190
An adventure was one of
the things that I wanted

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:04.530
to be present in my life.

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:06.693
I have a sense of adventure, you do too.

00:36:08.350 --> 00:36:11.840
But when you get super
busy, the opportunity

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:16.620
or the sort of scheduling
adventures can sometimes

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:17.860
go by the wayside.

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:20.710
And what I found is that I'm just

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:22.610
waiting for an opportunity for a vacation.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:24.280
Not anymore, but before quarantine,

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:29.280
it was like, "Jesse, let's
go spend a weekend in a hotel

00:36:29.327 --> 00:36:31.617
"and what we'll do is on Saturday,

00:36:31.617 --> 00:36:32.760
"we'll just lay by the pool."

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.600
And I kinda discovered this
like five or six years ago.

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:38.090
And I was like this laying next to a pool

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:40.763
and reading on vacation,
that's where it's at.

00:36:42.559 --> 00:36:44.140
But I started realizing
that we were doing things

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:48.100
like going to Cabo for a weekend

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:50.180
and laying next to the pool.

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:54.030
And of course, it's beautiful
and I love the company

00:36:57.196 --> 00:37:00.860
and great food, great drink, whatever.

00:37:00.860 --> 00:37:02.000
Starts to blur together.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:02.947
You're also like, "Hold on.

00:37:02.947 --> 00:37:05.167
"But right outside the
grounds of this hotel,

00:37:05.167 --> 00:37:09.516
"there's probably like a
cave that I could go into."

00:37:09.516 --> 00:37:11.680
You know what I'm saying?

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.447
The thing I told Jesse, I was like,

00:37:13.447 --> 00:37:15.617
"When we travel from now on,

00:37:15.617 --> 00:37:17.317
"I really wanna make sure that we've got

00:37:17.317 --> 00:37:20.040
"a balance of adventure and relaxation,"

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:21.337
because you don't wanna just go

00:37:21.337 --> 00:37:23.930
and just run yourself
ragged on a vacation,

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:26.280
but to really think about the
opportunities for adventure

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:29.200
that present themselves
in any given location.

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:31.873
Or adventure in your own backyard.

00:37:34.011 --> 00:37:35.610
You don't have to travel
to do it, not yet.

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:38.260
But I'm just saying,
that I haven't even seen

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:41.433
travel as primarily about adventure.

00:37:42.332 --> 00:37:46.320
We went to France a few years
ago for that film festival

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:47.153
and had it...

00:37:47.153 --> 00:37:48.070
We actually did,

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:49.990
what's the one thing you
remember about that trip?

00:37:49.990 --> 00:37:51.670
What's the one thing you
tell people about this trip?

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:52.573
The scooter.

00:37:53.594 --> 00:37:56.560
Almost dying on a scooter.
Yeah.

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:59.823
And it's like, 'cause we
made a choice for adventure.

00:38:01.222 --> 00:38:02.408
That was the right choice, man.

00:38:02.408 --> 00:38:05.320
That'll make your life short or long.

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:07.293
I didn't understand your science.

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:10.590
You might die, but it'll make
it make your life longer.

00:38:10.590 --> 00:38:12.807
Grace-Anna tweeted,

00:38:12.807 --> 00:38:14.837
"I turned 17 in just a few weeks

00:38:14.837 --> 00:38:16.607
"and I couldn't be more
ready for my birthday

00:38:16.607 --> 00:38:18.717
"and being one year closer to adulthood,

00:38:18.717 --> 00:38:21.237
"but I feel like being
so eager for adulthood

00:38:21.237 --> 00:38:23.017
"is almost wrong.

00:38:23.017 --> 00:38:26.837
"Everyone I talk to about
this says adulthood is awful.

00:38:26.837 --> 00:38:29.890
"How should I feel about
my impending adulthood?"

00:38:29.890 --> 00:38:31.373
Adulthood is awful.

00:38:33.687 --> 00:38:35.960
At 17?
Lily's 17.

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:36.793
It's like,

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:40.670
I understand you talk
about being cooped up.

00:38:40.670 --> 00:38:41.960
There's like a certain freedom,

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:45.803
like wanting to get on with your own life.

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:52.830
As a parent, her question
makes me just think about

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.370
how I look at my family
and starting to think

00:38:55.370 --> 00:39:00.370
as Lily's 17, Lincoln's 15, Lock is 16.

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:05.090
You starting to think about
them just months down the road

00:39:05.090 --> 00:39:06.840
of like leaving the house.

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:08.563
They're gonna be on their own.

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:12.460
It's definitely another
part of aging for me.

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:15.890
I'm kinda co-oping her
question for me as a parent but

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:21.237
I start to think about things like,

00:39:21.237 --> 00:39:25.608
when my kids get out of the house,

00:39:25.608 --> 00:39:28.403
what are they gonna come back to?

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:32.059
A van, apparently.

00:39:32.059 --> 00:39:34.570
They're gonna come back and
there's gonna be a foundation

00:39:34.570 --> 00:39:35.783
and a van on it.

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:41.920
Am I building a lifestyle
for me and Christie

00:39:42.430 --> 00:39:45.070
that is not dependent on kids

00:39:45.070 --> 00:39:47.410
and it doesn't revolve around our kids,

00:39:47.410 --> 00:39:52.410
but also build a future
and start to have dreams

00:39:52.660 --> 00:39:56.090
that my kids can enter back into

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:57.543
but it's not all about them?

00:39:59.770 --> 00:40:01.683
That's the next phase for us.

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:04.313
Lando's only 10 so.

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:07.290
The best way to keep
it about not about them

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:09.340
is as soon as they leave to go to college,

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:12.200
you turn their room into something else.

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:13.540
It doesn't even matter what it is.

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:15.755
It could be a gym, it could just be,

00:40:15.755 --> 00:40:19.812
just where all the socks
that you can't match are,

00:40:19.812 --> 00:40:20.645
you know what I'm saying?

00:40:20.645 --> 00:40:24.410
Just take their bed,
their desk, their posters,

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:27.807
get rid of it all because you
want to be very clear about,

00:40:27.807 --> 00:40:31.567
"You will not live here
after you graduate college.

00:40:31.567 --> 00:40:32.747
"So don't get any ideas

00:40:32.747 --> 00:40:36.430
"unless you wanna sleep on a
bunch of mismatched socks."

00:40:36.430 --> 00:40:37.320
That's my plan.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:38.660
But I started to think about like,

00:40:38.660 --> 00:40:40.150
am I gonna live in this house?

00:40:40.150 --> 00:40:45.150
Is this the house that
Lincoln is gonna come back to

00:40:45.550 --> 00:40:46.410
with his...

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:50.220
Is this the house that they
bring their partners back to?

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:52.027
Their significant
others, "Hey mom and dad,

00:40:52.027 --> 00:40:56.443
"you gotta meet Derek" I don't know.

00:40:57.330 --> 00:41:00.010
I'm predicting there's
gonna be a Derek involved.

00:41:00.010 --> 00:41:01.930
Jesse and I have,

00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:05.120
we've kind of made this decision that no,

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:07.880
that our current house
will not be that house.

00:41:07.880 --> 00:41:10.320
We're also like, we like change.

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:11.810
I'm surprised we haven't moved already,

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:16.113
but I think that at
this point, we're like,

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:20.020
we're committed to
staying in this location

00:41:20.020 --> 00:41:23.860
until Shepherd, which is like, he's 11.

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:26.970
So seven more years
until he goes to college.

00:41:26.970 --> 00:41:29.097
So maybe while they're
in college, we'd be like,

00:41:29.097 --> 00:41:30.847
"Hey, let's try something else."

00:41:33.280 --> 00:41:36.210
I haven't thought about
the child psychology,

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:40.753
because I know kids like to
come back to something familiar.

00:41:42.417 --> 00:41:43.250
So I don't know.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:44.520
I haven't thought about that aspect of it

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:47.550
Even seven more years in this house.

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:49.470
And I do start to think about that too.

00:41:49.470 --> 00:41:51.800
It's like, I know that

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:55.732
there'll be a lot more
memories in this house

00:41:55.732 --> 00:41:57.730
but there's also not,

00:41:57.730 --> 00:41:59.080
there's not a lot of room

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:02.023
for someone for additional
people to come back to.

00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:04.740
But I do start to think about that.

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:07.740
But to directly answer
Grace-Anna's question very quickly.

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:11.860
You should feel incredible about your,

00:42:11.860 --> 00:42:14.723
it's age 17 good, listen.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:20.600
The peak of your life is
right over the horizon.

00:42:20.656 --> 00:42:24.913
Listen, you've got right now at age 17,

00:42:24.913 --> 00:42:28.560
that there's a bunch of things
that are about to coincide.

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:33.560
Your level of passion for
things and your level of freedom

00:42:33.650 --> 00:42:38.650
are about to combine in a
beautiful synergistic explosion.

00:42:40.059 --> 00:42:42.542
And you need to realize that.

00:42:42.542 --> 00:42:45.720
Don't wish it away.

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:50.520
You have this incredible
opportunity to just

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:52.760
not have a whole lot of responsibility.

00:42:52.760 --> 00:42:53.830
Get a van.

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:55.663
Yes, get a van.

00:42:55.663 --> 00:42:57.080
Are we talking about a gap year here?

00:42:57.080 --> 00:42:57.950
Is that what you're talking about?

00:42:57.950 --> 00:42:59.250
No, no.

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:01.920
Well, first of all, gap years
are becoming very common

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:04.980
because of quarantine,
because of the coronavirus.

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:07.610
The whole world is in a gap year.

00:43:07.610 --> 00:43:10.340
Right but no, gap year, yes.

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:12.300
But I'm just talking about the fact that,

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:15.970
you're gonna feel like college is a lot,

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:18.173
but just let me tell you, it's not.

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:21.060
It's like don't,

00:43:21.060 --> 00:43:22.170
I'm not saying don't study.

00:43:22.170 --> 00:43:25.950
I'm just saying that
don't fall for the lie

00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:27.620
that it's a lot.

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:30.160
It's freedom, it's free.

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:31.450
And not everybody goes to college.

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:34.507
But what I'm saying is, before that

00:43:34.507 --> 00:43:38.480
you got this gap between
being under your parents' roof

00:43:38.480 --> 00:43:42.130
and then having a roof
with other people under it.

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:43.203
There's this window.

00:43:44.050 --> 00:43:46.460
That's the window of opportunity.

00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:47.293
You know what I'm saying?

00:43:47.293 --> 00:43:49.260
Just see it for what it is.

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:51.410
Just see it for what it is.

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:52.243
Enjoy it.

00:43:54.286 --> 00:43:55.453
Nouisxsweet.

00:43:57.160 --> 00:44:00.320
There's no name associated with
this, but that's the handle.

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:03.067
It looks like Louis but with an N.

00:44:03.067 --> 00:44:05.497
"What do you think is a turning
point for becoming an adult?

00:44:05.497 --> 00:44:08.097
"I'm currently 18 and my
life hasn't changed that much

00:44:08.097 --> 00:44:10.737
"these past years and I
can't see myself as an adult.

00:44:10.737 --> 00:44:12.367
"Is it getting a job.

00:44:12.367 --> 00:44:16.047
"a degree or is it just a matter of age?"

00:44:17.404 --> 00:44:22.404
Kennedy, @kennedygerow, kind
of adds to this question.

00:44:25.037 --> 00:44:26.267
"At what point in your life did you feel

00:44:26.267 --> 00:44:28.387
"like you actually had
a good handle on things?

00:44:28.387 --> 00:44:31.327
"I just graduated college
and I will start my first,

00:44:31.327 --> 00:44:35.280
"big girl job soon and
I'm very overwhelmed."

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:37.372
It's like trying to figure out

00:44:37.372 --> 00:44:38.890
when are you an adult

00:44:38.890 --> 00:44:41.590
and when do you feel like
you have a handle on things?

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:44.620
I've said before that...

00:44:44.620 --> 00:44:45.610
Yeah, Christie's back there.

00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:48.157
She's mouthing "never".

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:50.400
Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:51.530
I mean, I think I've thought before.

00:44:51.530 --> 00:44:53.640
I felt like I've said before that,

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:57.010
I feel like I'm an adult
when I had responsibility,

00:44:57.010 --> 00:45:00.610
definitely having kids made me feel like,

00:45:00.610 --> 00:45:03.840
oh, well I'm not the kid
because now I have one.

00:45:03.840 --> 00:45:05.650
That must make me the adult.

00:45:05.650 --> 00:45:08.720
But it was something that I
kinda had to stop and derive.

00:45:08.720 --> 00:45:10.570
Well, yes.

00:45:10.570 --> 00:45:15.200
To me, I think the moment
that you become an adult

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:18.200
is when you have the realization

00:45:18.200 --> 00:45:21.810
that no one has a handle on things.

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:24.210
Like it's the moment in which you are,

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:29.210
which is like all of a
sudden, you look around

00:45:29.298 --> 00:45:30.348
and you're like, "Oh,

00:45:31.337 --> 00:45:34.367
"They also don't know what
the hell they're doing.

00:45:34.367 --> 00:45:36.267
"This is great.

00:45:36.267 --> 00:45:38.447
"They're all faking it.

00:45:38.447 --> 00:45:41.700
"They're all faking that
they've got it figured out."

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:43.810
That's the moment you become an adult

00:45:43.810 --> 00:45:45.680
because when you're a child,

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:48.820
you look up usually because you're short

00:45:48.820 --> 00:45:52.020
and you see these adults and
they just, they seem like,

00:45:52.020 --> 00:45:53.050
I mean, they're moving and then...

00:45:53.050 --> 00:45:55.380
Their shirts are total direction britches.

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:57.910
And they're like cranking up cars

00:45:57.910 --> 00:46:00.400
and like jogging and bringing in food.

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:03.790
They're doing lots of
things that seem purposeful,

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:06.100
that make you feel stable.

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:08.890
But then when you become
that person and you're like,

00:46:08.890 --> 00:46:11.793
I'm kinda just doing what
they did, but hold on.

00:46:12.687 --> 00:46:14.470
I don't really know what I'm doing.

00:46:14.470 --> 00:46:15.360
I feel overwhelmed.

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:16.650
I don't have a handle on things.

00:46:16.650 --> 00:46:20.220
That's the realization, that's
when you become an adult.

00:46:20.220 --> 00:46:24.270
Well, Lisa @BleedBlueBurgun
tweeted, one of these

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:25.773
usernames are crazy.

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:27.867
Lisa said, "I think this quote

00:46:27.867 --> 00:46:30.547
"would be a cool topic
point on getting older.

00:46:30.547 --> 00:46:32.617
"This is from Alden Nowlan."

00:46:33.470 --> 00:46:35.487
Nowlan never heard of this guy, have you?

00:46:35.487 --> 00:46:39.927
"The day the child realizes
that all adults are imperfect,

00:46:39.927 --> 00:46:42.057
"he becomes an adolescent.

00:46:42.057 --> 00:46:46.170
"The day he forgives them,
he becomes an adult."

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:48.056
That's an eloquent way of saying

00:46:48.056 --> 00:46:49.260
what we're talking about.
Same thing we were saying,

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:51.710
that there's a level of maturity

00:46:52.910 --> 00:46:55.420
associated with realizing that no one is,

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:56.720
everyone's imperfect.

00:46:56.720 --> 00:46:59.170
Nobody's got everything figured out.

00:46:59.170 --> 00:47:01.320
No one makes the right decision.

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:04.490
Everybody's in need of forgiveness.

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:07.053
And if you can give that,

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:10.700
I think that's a,

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:12.300
I think that's a good measuring stick

00:47:12.300 --> 00:47:15.563
for adulthood, for maturity.

00:47:16.520 --> 00:47:19.650
If you can forgive people and
probably forgive yourself too

00:47:19.650 --> 00:47:21.337
and give yourself an end to say,

00:47:21.337 --> 00:47:24.967
"Hey, we're all just, we're all just,

00:47:24.967 --> 00:47:29.967
"we're all stumbling forward
in time towards our own demise

00:47:30.247 --> 00:47:32.450
"but we might as well live
it up in the meantime."

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:34.180
'Cause another aspect of it,

00:47:34.180 --> 00:47:37.240
I don't remember exactly
the moment that this hit me,

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:42.240
but there was a moment
in which I found myself

00:47:42.510 --> 00:47:47.259
living through a personal
moment in my life

00:47:47.259 --> 00:47:52.259
that coincided with the
first memory of my parents.

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:54.850
Does that make sense?

00:47:54.850 --> 00:47:57.040
So, when I started realizing, like,

00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:59.630
I remember like seeing
my parents be parents,

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:01.470
there was a moment that I can remember

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:04.140
my parents doing parental things.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:06.160
And then there's a moment in which

00:48:06.160 --> 00:48:09.230
you see yourself doing the same thing

00:48:09.230 --> 00:48:12.070
and that's when you can begin to get

00:48:12.070 --> 00:48:13.310
to that point of forgiveness.

00:48:13.310 --> 00:48:15.870
You're like, "Damn, it's
hard to be a parent."

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:17.930
You had sympathy for your own parents

00:48:17.930 --> 00:48:19.820
and even though it doesn't help,

00:48:19.820 --> 00:48:21.550
I tell my 16 year old this all the time.

00:48:21.550 --> 00:48:24.290
It's like, "I can't wait
till you have your own kids."

00:48:24.290 --> 00:48:25.307
I mean, that's

00:48:25.307 --> 00:48:27.410
a pointless thing to say.
Cliche.

00:48:27.410 --> 00:48:29.600
And I shouldn't say it,
I say it in frustration,

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:32.920
but it's also true because
there will come a time

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:36.150
I'm hoping that there will
be a phone call or an email,

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:37.697
or at least a text that is just like,

00:48:37.697 --> 00:48:39.832
"Okay, dad, I get it."

00:48:41.231 --> 00:48:42.211
You know what I'm saying?

00:48:42.211 --> 00:48:44.640
And I think that's the moment.

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:46.620
That's another way to
kind of gauge adulthood.

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:47.710
You don't have to be a parent.

00:48:47.710 --> 00:48:50.860
It could be a stage of life

00:48:50.860 --> 00:48:52.580
where you can relate
to another generation.

00:48:52.580 --> 00:48:56.420
Did you ever have that
moment with your parents

00:48:56.420 --> 00:48:58.200
where you were like, "You
know what, I get it."

00:48:58.200 --> 00:48:59.603
It's funny today,

00:49:00.580 --> 00:49:04.100
I talked to my mom, she
wished me happy birthday

00:49:04.100 --> 00:49:05.847
and she was like,

00:49:05.847 --> 00:49:08.300
"Well, I wanna know, do
you feel old as dirt?"

00:49:08.300 --> 00:49:10.810
And I was like, I laughed.

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:13.383
And she was like, "When I turned 42,

00:49:14.437 --> 00:49:17.247
"I remembered that you told me,"

00:49:17.247 --> 00:49:21.374
"Man, mom, you're old as dirt."

00:49:21.374 --> 00:49:24.290
She held on to that.
"So now that you're 42,

00:49:24.290 --> 00:49:25.460
do you feel old as dirt?"

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:28.330
And she said she remembered it.

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:30.167
She's been waiting all these years.

00:49:30.167 --> 00:49:31.663
She's been waiting all these years.

00:49:32.670 --> 00:49:34.110
I don't remember saying that to her.

00:49:34.110 --> 00:49:36.750
I'm sure it was a joke.

00:49:36.750 --> 00:49:37.950
She basically said it was a joke.

00:49:37.950 --> 00:49:38.833
But, do you...

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:43.960
There is an aspect of aging that is,

00:49:43.960 --> 00:49:46.253
you talk to your parents differently.

00:49:47.350 --> 00:49:50.250
Was there a point where
you, just the way that

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:54.327
you're waiting for Lock
to text you and say,

00:49:54.327 --> 00:49:55.570
"You know what, I get it."

00:49:55.570 --> 00:49:57.740
Do you recall giving that to your parents?

00:49:57.740 --> 00:50:00.363
The way that that comes up for me is,

00:50:01.890 --> 00:50:03.850
I don't do this all the time,

00:50:03.850 --> 00:50:08.776
but I'm not necessarily
great at saying it.

00:50:08.776 --> 00:50:11.180
I'm pretty good at writing it.

00:50:11.180 --> 00:50:15.940
And so, in a number of
cards over the years,

00:50:15.940 --> 00:50:17.810
whether it's Father's Day, Mother's Day,

00:50:17.810 --> 00:50:19.293
birthday or whatever,

00:50:21.130 --> 00:50:22.610
and again, I'm not super
consistent in this,

00:50:22.610 --> 00:50:25.870
but I've definitely have specific memories

00:50:25.870 --> 00:50:27.560
of sitting down and being,

00:50:27.560 --> 00:50:31.900
I'm going to write how
much I appreciate that,

00:50:33.700 --> 00:50:34.820
how my mom mothered me,

00:50:34.820 --> 00:50:38.283
how my father fathered
me because I get it.

00:50:39.940 --> 00:50:43.000
Understanding how meaningful it was

00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:46.590
to have my dad present
for all my sporting events

00:50:46.590 --> 00:50:48.307
and stuff like that and
growing up and realizing,

00:50:48.307 --> 00:50:50.890
"Damn, that's really hard to do."

00:50:50.890 --> 00:50:55.027
That took intention and sacrifice.

00:50:55.027 --> 00:50:59.300
So acknowledging that in
cards is what you've done?

00:50:59.300 --> 00:51:01.610
Yeah, I'm not saying we
haven't talked about it,

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:03.620
but I'm saying that I, in some fashion,

00:51:03.620 --> 00:51:05.180
even though I'm not the best communicator,

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:07.306
I have communicated that to
them as I've grown older.

00:51:07.306 --> 00:51:10.470
I remember one time when I was in college,

00:51:10.470 --> 00:51:13.180
my dad had a camper and
he took it to Jordan Lake

00:51:13.180 --> 00:51:15.340
and me and my dad,

00:51:15.340 --> 00:51:18.130
this was like the first
time ever that me and my dad

00:51:18.130 --> 00:51:21.310
like did a camping trip,
like sleepover thing

00:51:21.310 --> 00:51:24.000
where it was just the two of us.

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:28.266
And I remember we were sitting
out there at Jordan Lake

00:51:28.266 --> 00:51:31.660
and we cracked open a beer,
we like shared a beer.

00:51:31.660 --> 00:51:33.500
Well, we each had our own beer.

00:51:33.500 --> 00:51:37.660
And I just remember that
being a milestone moment.

00:51:37.660 --> 00:51:39.150
I remember being nervous going.

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:42.279
I was like, "I'm driving out
here and meeting my dad."

00:51:42.279 --> 00:51:43.960
I never lived with my dad

00:51:43.960 --> 00:51:46.770
except in like visitation situations.

00:51:46.770 --> 00:51:50.644
But it was really cool
because like all of a sudden

00:51:50.644 --> 00:51:53.620
we were two guys doing things that

00:51:53.620 --> 00:51:56.320
we would both do with our friends,

00:51:56.320 --> 00:51:57.690
but we were doing it with each other

00:51:57.690 --> 00:51:59.940
and we were having a conversation

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:02.900
that we would each have with a friend.

00:52:02.900 --> 00:52:06.620
And I mean, that was a long
time coming from me and my dad,

00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:09.010
because we never lived together.

00:52:09.010 --> 00:52:12.500
There are elements of
that with all of my kids,

00:52:12.500 --> 00:52:15.103
like I try to speak to them.

00:52:16.420 --> 00:52:18.880
I talk to them about
things and I talk to them

00:52:18.880 --> 00:52:21.970
in ways that I would talk to you sometimes

00:52:21.970 --> 00:52:23.570
depending on the subject matter.

00:52:26.030 --> 00:52:29.210
There are these parents who,

00:52:29.210 --> 00:52:32.100
they never stop baby talking their kids,

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:33.483
even when they're adults.

00:52:35.227 --> 00:52:38.150
I know of people, I'm not close to people,

00:52:38.150 --> 00:52:41.490
but I've observed people
who would still talk

00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:44.198
kind of like baby talk to their,

00:52:44.198 --> 00:52:47.330
their daughter or son that's in their 20s.

00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:48.740
What?
Yeah.

00:52:48.740 --> 00:52:50.560
I'm thinking of one person in particular

00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:52.390
that you, but you don't know them.

00:52:52.390 --> 00:52:53.570
We need to talk about this after,

00:52:53.570 --> 00:52:54.420
because I don't know

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:56.170
There's parents who never change

00:52:56.170 --> 00:52:57.960
how they talk to their children

00:52:58.852 --> 00:53:01.630
and that's just not good.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:04.340
I never baby talked either of my kids.

00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:05.660
I just baby talk the dog.

00:53:05.660 --> 00:53:08.070
I figured that she can take
it for her entire life.

00:53:08.070 --> 00:53:10.490
But I look forward to having

00:53:10.490 --> 00:53:13.930
an adult to adult
relationship with my kids.

00:53:13.930 --> 00:53:16.270
That's why I like to
picture them once they leave

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:19.360
coming back and whether
it's here or somewhere else,

00:53:19.360 --> 00:53:22.760
like I wanna create an environment where

00:53:22.760 --> 00:53:26.763
they wanna be a part of
me and Christie's lives.

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:30.713
Well, you know, the way to do that.

00:53:30.713 --> 00:53:34.610
The way to do that is...
Probably a beach house.

00:53:34.610 --> 00:53:38.410
Yes, you get, either get a beach house.

00:53:38.410 --> 00:53:43.030
If we're so blessed to
be able to afford one,

00:53:43.030 --> 00:53:45.790
or you rent a beach house

00:53:45.790 --> 00:53:49.810
and taking your adult
children on vacation,

00:53:49.810 --> 00:53:50.930
that's how you keep them.

00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:52.770
That's how you keep 'em around

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:55.870
because they are not
gonna be able to afford

00:53:55.870 --> 00:53:57.010
their own vacation.

00:53:57.010 --> 00:54:00.120
They're all going into this world

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:03.170
that's gonna be all the robots
are gonna have the jobs.

00:54:03.170 --> 00:54:06.160
They're gonna wanna go on
vacation with you forever.

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:06.993
They're gonna want it.

00:54:06.993 --> 00:54:10.420
They're going to wanna like
take the hibernation pill

00:54:10.420 --> 00:54:12.410
and then only wake up for hibernation

00:54:12.410 --> 00:54:13.560
to go--
For a vacation.

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:17.090
With me and then just hibernate again.

00:54:17.090 --> 00:54:19.360
And I think that what we're gonna do,

00:54:19.360 --> 00:54:22.500
and we'll still be making
content on some level.

00:54:22.500 --> 00:54:24.697
Maybe we'll still be making this podcast.

00:54:24.697 --> 00:54:27.800
I think that we need to commit right now

00:54:27.800 --> 00:54:31.480
that we're going to be the parents

00:54:31.480 --> 00:54:35.003
that they want to go on vacation with.

00:54:35.890 --> 00:54:36.780
You know what I'm saying?

00:54:36.780 --> 00:54:37.957
You gotta think about it.

00:54:37.957 --> 00:54:40.900
There needs to be a survey or something.

00:54:40.900 --> 00:54:44.450
We gotta do like a kid's
focus group to be like,

00:54:44.450 --> 00:54:48.760
what makes going on vacation
with people miserable

00:54:48.760 --> 00:54:51.720
and make sure that we're not
the ones contributing to that.

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:53.943
Whatever that is, let's commit to that.

00:54:54.920 --> 00:54:56.750
Because somebody has got the ability

00:54:56.750 --> 00:54:59.470
to pay for your vacation,
doesn't make the vacation fun.

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:00.810
No.

00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:02.700
Those are two completely different things.

00:55:02.700 --> 00:55:07.453
All right, Erika with a K,
very active mythical beast.

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:10.100
She's got a...

00:55:10.100 --> 00:55:12.510
Her question was so intense and so long

00:55:12.510 --> 00:55:14.017
that it took two tweets.

00:55:14.017 --> 00:55:16.527
"Looking back at myself in my twenties

00:55:16.527 --> 00:55:18.687
"and even early thirties
and realizing that

00:55:18.687 --> 00:55:21.247
"many of my opinions and
viewpoints were still being formed

00:55:21.247 --> 00:55:22.807
"by my parent.

00:55:22.807 --> 00:55:25.547
"I feel like I didn't start
forming independent thoughts

00:55:25.547 --> 00:55:27.017
"until later in life.

00:55:27.017 --> 00:55:30.277
"When do you think you start
truly having opinions and views

00:55:30.277 --> 00:55:33.537
"that wouldn't necessarily
be approved by your parents?

00:55:33.537 --> 00:55:35.407
"Do you think the need for approval

00:55:35.407 --> 00:55:38.127
"from such major influences, parents

00:55:38.127 --> 00:55:41.980
"slows down progressive
changes in societal issues?"

00:55:41.980 --> 00:55:44.650
Whoa, this is a big one to get
into right here at the end.

00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:45.750
Let's just say, yes.

00:55:46.790 --> 00:55:49.977
She asked me, that's my answer, yes.

00:55:49.977 --> 00:55:52.070
#EarBiscuits, let's know what you think.

00:55:52.070 --> 00:55:53.270
No, we're gonna answer it.

00:55:53.270 --> 00:55:56.590
So, you know, when we
talked about the last years,

00:55:56.590 --> 00:55:59.020
we kinda told our life story
and that we were raised

00:55:59.020 --> 00:56:01.820
in a very particular
sort of way of thinking

00:56:01.820 --> 00:56:05.783
and we don't think that
way exactly anymore.

00:56:07.185 --> 00:56:09.550
And first of all, that's
a super common thing

00:56:09.550 --> 00:56:13.330
not just today with us, been a
super common thing throughout

00:56:13.330 --> 00:56:15.070
the history of the world,

00:56:15.070 --> 00:56:17.290
especially post-industrial revolution,

00:56:17.290 --> 00:56:19.840
there's lots of societal
generational changes

00:56:19.840 --> 00:56:21.860
that take place pretty quickly.

00:56:21.860 --> 00:56:24.757
And the older generations
is like "Back in my day,

00:56:24.757 --> 00:56:26.590
"we didn't think like this."

00:56:26.590 --> 00:56:31.040
But so anyway, and also
most child psychologists

00:56:31.040 --> 00:56:36.040
suggest that a child sees a structure,

00:56:36.300 --> 00:56:41.150
and most kids need to sort of
rebel through that structure,

00:56:41.150 --> 00:56:42.900
even if they kinda come back to it.

00:56:44.340 --> 00:56:47.007
And Jesse and I were talking
about our personalities

00:56:47.007 --> 00:56:51.420
and we are both rule followers.

00:56:51.420 --> 00:56:54.270
I'm a three on the
Enneagram, I'm a performer,

00:56:54.270 --> 00:56:57.920
but one of the great ways to
kinda exercise your threeness

00:56:57.920 --> 00:57:00.520
is to recognize

00:57:00.520 --> 00:57:04.617
how to succeed within a
framework and then be like,

00:57:04.617 --> 00:57:06.027
"Oh, I can do that, that's what I did."

00:57:06.027 --> 00:57:07.110
"I did well in school,

00:57:07.110 --> 00:57:09.147
"I did well in sports and I was a good kid

00:57:09.147 --> 00:57:10.437
"that made my parents proud."

00:57:10.437 --> 00:57:11.940
It was really good at that.

00:57:11.940 --> 00:57:13.330
Jesse's a two on the Enneagram,

00:57:13.330 --> 00:57:15.630
which is not about achieving,

00:57:15.630 --> 00:57:18.590
but it's more about
helping people do things

00:57:18.590 --> 00:57:22.440
and basically being there for people,

00:57:22.440 --> 00:57:25.150
but in the same way that
she's a rule follower.

00:57:25.150 --> 00:57:28.410
So we got well into
adulthood, into our 30s,

00:57:28.410 --> 00:57:31.593
still holding very tightly
to basically the things

00:57:31.593 --> 00:57:34.270
that we have been taught in
our parents that taught us.

00:57:34.270 --> 00:57:36.420
But we were also then talking about

00:57:36.420 --> 00:57:38.090
a guy that we went to high school with,

00:57:38.090 --> 00:57:41.140
I'm not gonna name his name,
that was a friend of ours

00:57:42.590 --> 00:57:44.880
who also went to church with us.

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:47.890
But then all of a
sudden, you remember this

00:57:47.890 --> 00:57:51.780
prom one year, junior year or senior year,

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:54.360
he showed up dressed,
In a dress.

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:56.683
But not just dressed as a woman,

00:57:57.690 --> 00:58:01.550
like he had on makeup
and he had done his hair

00:58:01.550 --> 00:58:04.240
and it almost was like so well done

00:58:04.240 --> 00:58:06.950
that the first thing I thought
was, who's the new girl?

00:58:06.950 --> 00:58:08.650
I remember thinking that.

00:58:08.650 --> 00:58:11.420
And then the second thing I thought was

00:58:11.420 --> 00:58:15.880
that's so-and-so so-and-so
and with the time I was like,

00:58:15.880 --> 00:58:16.880
Oh, that's a funny joke.

00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:19.120
Like, I wasn't like, I
can't believe he did that.

00:58:19.120 --> 00:58:20.500
I remember just thinking that, like,

00:58:20.500 --> 00:58:23.954
that was really bold and very funny.

00:58:23.954 --> 00:58:26.170
He really pulled that off,

00:58:26.170 --> 00:58:30.699
but I didn't stop and
think, why did he do that?

00:58:30.699 --> 00:58:35.260
As we grew up a little
bit, I realized that, oh...

00:58:35.260 --> 00:58:36.610
He was making a statement.

00:58:36.610 --> 00:58:39.510
He checked out.

00:58:39.510 --> 00:58:41.940
He rebelled against the system

00:58:41.940 --> 00:58:43.630
that he saw in place at the time.

00:58:43.630 --> 00:58:45.680
He just did it in high school

00:58:45.680 --> 00:58:46.933
and then he went out to college,

00:58:46.933 --> 00:58:49.310
and became an adult who was somebody

00:58:49.310 --> 00:58:52.640
that didn't hold to the same
worldview as his parents.

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:56.090
But it took me to get to in my 30s,

00:58:56.090 --> 00:58:59.690
before I began to
investigate the worldview.

00:58:59.690 --> 00:59:03.730
And I'm not saying that
this isn't really a truth,

00:59:03.730 --> 00:59:05.500
non true, whether it's true or not,

00:59:05.500 --> 00:59:06.333
that's not what I'm...

00:59:06.333 --> 00:59:08.040
We talked about that a
lot in the last years.

00:59:08.040 --> 00:59:11.690
This is more about like
the way that you think

00:59:11.690 --> 00:59:13.980
and the way that you see the world,

00:59:13.980 --> 00:59:18.640
have you ever made it personal?

00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:21.390
You may just you may transfer
the same exact worldview

00:59:21.390 --> 00:59:24.360
that was handed to you by your
parents and make it personal

00:59:24.360 --> 00:59:26.913
and actually passionately
interact with it.

00:59:28.670 --> 00:59:31.170
But usually the process of actually

00:59:31.170 --> 00:59:34.090
wanting to kinda take
control and make it your own,

00:59:34.090 --> 00:59:35.410
one of two things happen,

00:59:35.410 --> 00:59:37.970
you grab hold of it and
it slips out of your hand.

00:59:37.970 --> 00:59:40.097
You realize that's not for
me or you grab hold of it

00:59:40.097 --> 00:59:42.040
and you realize that it
fits your hands perfectly

00:59:42.040 --> 00:59:43.600
and you adopt it.

00:59:43.600 --> 00:59:46.760
But different personalities
make that decision

00:59:46.760 --> 00:59:48.220
at different times.

00:59:48.220 --> 00:59:51.180
I kinda got all the way through my 20s,

00:59:51.180 --> 00:59:54.320
still in a very performance
oriented mindset

00:59:54.320 --> 00:59:58.150
where it was about
making my parents proud.

00:59:58.150 --> 00:59:59.500
And that's not every,

00:59:59.500 --> 01:00:02.720
I think Erika is saying that
she's the same kind of person.

01:00:02.720 --> 01:00:04.610
My wife's the same kind of person,

01:00:04.610 --> 01:00:07.100
but a lot of people, including the guy

01:00:07.100 --> 01:00:09.259
we're talking about who we
went to high school with,

01:00:09.259 --> 01:00:10.950
it happened earlier for him.

01:00:10.950 --> 01:00:12.923
Yeah, I think for me too it was.

01:00:15.960 --> 01:00:17.660
If you tell me the right way to do it

01:00:17.660 --> 01:00:20.136
and enough people agree, then it's just,

01:00:20.136 --> 01:00:24.320
this is the safe, secure
path and I'm gonna,

01:00:24.320 --> 01:00:25.370
I can feel okay.

01:00:25.370 --> 01:00:28.000
I can feel confident and it's,

01:00:28.000 --> 01:00:32.650
I didn't have this sense of
needing to discover myself

01:00:32.650 --> 01:00:37.650
or express myself in a way
that was counter to anything

01:00:39.570 --> 01:00:42.523
that I was, the plans that I was adopting.

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:47.700
I just wanna feel okay.

01:00:47.700 --> 01:00:49.843
I just wanna feel safe.

01:00:51.630 --> 01:00:54.350
I don't wanna feel like

01:00:54.350 --> 01:00:59.350
I'm out here on a limb
trying to figure this out.

01:01:00.029 --> 01:01:02.980
I just didn't have, I
didn't have that drive.

01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:05.330
I had another drive, I
had to drive for security.

01:01:05.330 --> 01:01:08.363
And so yeah, it was,

01:01:10.650 --> 01:01:14.390
I think it did take a long time for me

01:01:14.390 --> 01:01:18.090
to start to figure out who I was for me

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:19.413
and not for anybody else.

01:01:20.728 --> 01:01:25.728
And then, we got married so young

01:01:26.730 --> 01:01:28.993
that you transfer from,

01:01:29.990 --> 01:01:33.670
okay, this is the family
that I grew up in.

01:01:33.670 --> 01:01:37.180
This is a system that works there to...

01:01:37.180 --> 01:01:40.370
Now this is the family that I've formed.

01:01:40.370 --> 01:01:44.510
And I gotta meet Christie's expectations.

01:01:44.510 --> 01:01:46.150
She's gotta meet my expectations.

01:01:46.150 --> 01:01:50.143
It's like, you go from one
to another, at least we did.

01:01:52.180 --> 01:01:56.117
Yeah, well into the second
part of the question about,

01:01:56.117 --> 01:01:57.747
"Do you think this slows down

01:01:57.747 --> 01:01:59.931
"progressive changes in societal issues?"

01:01:59.931 --> 01:02:04.590
Definitely it does, but, and I'm not

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:06.410
listen, I didn't study this.

01:02:06.410 --> 01:02:09.000
This is total Rhett
pulling it out of his ass,

01:02:09.000 --> 01:02:11.053
which is a good bit of what I say,

01:02:12.480 --> 01:02:14.670
but my theory on this,

01:02:14.670 --> 01:02:18.203
the way that I think about
like societal progression,

01:02:21.578 --> 01:02:25.510
I think one of the big changes
in worldview that happened,

01:02:25.510 --> 01:02:27.990
like when I was a evangelical Christian,

01:02:27.990 --> 01:02:31.610
I saw the world as getting
progressively worse

01:02:31.610 --> 01:02:35.930
'cause that's consistent with eschatology.

01:02:35.930 --> 01:02:38.110
The idea that things are gonna slowly get

01:02:38.110 --> 01:02:41.800
progressively worse and then
Jesus is gonna come back.

01:02:41.800 --> 01:02:45.360
Now that I don't believe that anymore

01:02:45.360 --> 01:02:47.470
and I'm more progressive in my mindset.

01:02:47.470 --> 01:02:51.910
I actually think that the
world is getting better.

01:02:51.910 --> 01:02:55.110
And I actually think if
you read Steven Pinker's

01:02:55.110 --> 01:02:58.190
latest book, I think the
facts are on the side of it.

01:02:58.190 --> 01:03:00.210
Actually, even though
it seems more chaotic

01:03:00.210 --> 01:03:01.763
and you're getting a lot more
information that makes it

01:03:01.763 --> 01:03:05.623
seem even right now with riots
happening throughout the,

01:03:06.980 --> 01:03:08.047
throughout the nation, you may be,

01:03:08.047 --> 01:03:09.800
"Oh everything's going crazy."

01:03:09.800 --> 01:03:12.460
Well, in reality, basically every marker

01:03:12.460 --> 01:03:15.820
that you can measure, society
is getting better over time.

01:03:15.820 --> 01:03:17.660
So I think the facts are on the side of

01:03:17.660 --> 01:03:18.810
things are progressing.

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:22.650
But what I've always said is that

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:28.810
stretching progression like you know,

01:03:28.810 --> 01:03:32.130
social progressive stuff is like

01:03:32.130 --> 01:03:36.233
stretching the fabric of society.

01:03:37.315 --> 01:03:41.360
And it eventually fills
in and you stretch it.

01:03:41.360 --> 01:03:44.270
And I think that people only have

01:03:44.270 --> 01:03:47.150
so much capacity for change.

01:03:47.150 --> 01:03:50.680
It's like you pull it,
you pull it too hard

01:03:50.680 --> 01:03:52.830
and sometimes it rips and
then you've gotta stitch it

01:03:52.830 --> 01:03:54.979
and you gotta keep pulling.

01:03:54.979 --> 01:03:57.050
I believe that you keep pushing things

01:03:57.050 --> 01:03:58.120
in the right direction.

01:03:58.120 --> 01:04:03.013
And to me, it's just a good
segue to zoom out and say,

01:04:05.747 --> 01:04:07.810
I feel like what's going on right now.

01:04:07.810 --> 01:04:12.450
We're at such a pivotal
moment in history and

01:04:14.640 --> 01:04:18.460
it's such an important moment in time that

01:04:18.460 --> 01:04:20.900
we can choose to be on the sidelines

01:04:20.900 --> 01:04:23.060
be part of the problem,
be a part of the solutions

01:04:23.060 --> 01:04:26.180
be a part of the evolution,

01:04:26.180 --> 01:04:28.820
the positive evolution of society

01:04:28.820 --> 01:04:33.520
and I do have hope, to
bring the full circle

01:04:33.520 --> 01:04:38.520
as we listen, and as we have
open minds and open hearts,

01:04:42.010 --> 01:04:46.210
that it's a privilege
to have the opportunity

01:04:46.210 --> 01:04:49.390
to be a part of what's happening now.

01:04:49.390 --> 01:04:51.920
And it's scary

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:56.453
and there's no clear path
forward on a number of fronts.

01:04:59.454 --> 01:05:01.870
What you think about racial injustice,

01:05:01.870 --> 01:05:03.043
what you think about the environment,

01:05:03.043 --> 01:05:06.630
what you think about this pandemic,

01:05:06.630 --> 01:05:08.183
you can add things to the list.

01:05:14.220 --> 01:05:17.487
But we're a part of it
and we can impact it

01:05:19.270 --> 01:05:21.783
and we can do that stretching.

01:05:25.520 --> 01:05:29.260
I'm choosing to find hope in that

01:05:30.233 --> 01:05:32.240
and to find a way to be a part of it

01:05:32.240 --> 01:05:37.240
and to move forward and not
just try to escape or ignore,

01:05:39.270 --> 01:05:43.820
or just get old.

01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:46.630
Yeah, well, and I think
that you were talking about

01:05:46.630 --> 01:05:49.760
earlier the aspect of
feeling like you're standing

01:05:49.760 --> 01:05:52.400
on the sidelines sometimes.

01:05:52.400 --> 01:05:54.820
And I think everybody
probably has a tendency

01:05:54.820 --> 01:05:56.120
to feel that way.

01:05:56.120 --> 01:05:59.210
The way that I've kind
of seen my role in this

01:05:59.210 --> 01:06:04.210
stretching of societal fabric
over the course of my life

01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:05.493
is that first of all,

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:09.900
my personality is one who is naturally

01:06:09.900 --> 01:06:11.520
gonna grab onto the blanket.

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:13.410
I'm going to grab onto the fabric.

01:06:13.410 --> 01:06:16.090
That's just the kinda way I live my life.

01:06:16.090 --> 01:06:21.090
And I spent a good portion of my life

01:06:21.840 --> 01:06:25.384
pulling on one side of that fabric,

01:06:25.384 --> 01:06:27.847
trying to keep it in place.

01:06:27.847 --> 01:06:29.741
And I feel like one of
the things that I'm doing

01:06:29.741 --> 01:06:33.390
in the second half of my life is saying,

01:06:33.390 --> 01:06:36.080
"I actually feel like I was wrong

01:06:36.080 --> 01:06:38.574
"and I'm moving to the
other side of the blanket

01:06:38.574 --> 01:06:40.621
"and I'm pulling on it."

01:06:40.621 --> 01:06:43.326
And that's kinda the way that I see

01:06:43.326 --> 01:06:45.270
the two halves of my life
and the way I've kinda

01:06:45.270 --> 01:06:50.180
contributed to what Erika
is kind of talking about,

01:06:50.180 --> 01:06:52.583
these bigger societal issues.

01:06:54.550 --> 01:06:55.920
And so in some ways I kind of feel like

01:06:55.920 --> 01:06:59.250
I'm making up for things and that's just,

01:06:59.250 --> 01:07:00.640
I don't know if that's right or wrong,

01:07:00.640 --> 01:07:05.070
but that's how I interact with it.

01:07:05.070 --> 01:07:08.210
And I don't think, and I do think that

01:07:08.210 --> 01:07:09.720
there was sort of this middle period where

01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:11.490
it was kinda like, all right,

01:07:11.490 --> 01:07:13.370
I'm gonna let go of the blanket.

01:07:13.370 --> 01:07:15.870
I'm letting go of the fabric
everybody's pulling on.

01:07:15.870 --> 01:07:18.130
I'm gonna just watch for a little bit.

01:07:18.130 --> 01:07:19.707
And then once I was like, "Okay,

01:07:19.707 --> 01:07:22.400
"I think I know what
direction I wanna pull."

01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:24.070
I've just been pulling it harder.

01:07:24.070 --> 01:07:25.320
In the context of aging,

01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:27.933
we've talked about being engaged.

01:07:29.861 --> 01:07:34.700
You can let life pass you by, as they say,

01:07:34.700 --> 01:07:37.100
you can let life happen to you

01:07:38.154 --> 01:07:39.980
or you can happen to life.

01:07:39.980 --> 01:07:42.200
I think that's the thing that I want to,

01:07:42.200 --> 01:07:44.490
I want to be engaged.

01:07:44.490 --> 01:07:46.920
I want to be happening to life

01:07:46.920 --> 01:07:50.970
and not life just happening to me.

01:07:50.970 --> 01:07:54.500
Life throws a lot at
us and there's things,

01:07:54.500 --> 01:07:56.823
but when you respond,

01:08:00.758 --> 01:08:03.340
we do have the ability to engage.

01:08:03.340 --> 01:08:06.190
And I think that that's
again, that's exciting

01:08:06.190 --> 01:08:09.360
and that's a place where
there's hope in that.

01:08:09.360 --> 01:08:11.350
Yeah, well, and the last thing I'll say is

01:08:11.350 --> 01:08:14.340
I think that the biggest difference

01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:17.603
with the way that I
engage about these things,

01:08:20.483 --> 01:08:24.193
and specifically like the
issue of racial injustice,

01:08:25.250 --> 01:08:28.340
I think that I spent a
good portion of my life

01:08:28.340 --> 01:08:32.863
thinking about what I had
to protect personally.

01:08:34.310 --> 01:08:36.630
Like what were my rights and my interests

01:08:36.630 --> 01:08:39.250
and how do I hold tightly to those

01:08:39.250 --> 01:08:41.073
and how do I protect those?

01:08:41.960 --> 01:08:44.420
And I think one of the biggest
changes that has taken place

01:08:44.420 --> 01:08:49.150
over the past decade is trying
to think a little bit less

01:08:49.150 --> 01:08:52.480
about what I have and what my rights are

01:08:52.480 --> 01:08:55.840
and think about people who
have been marginalized,

01:08:55.840 --> 01:08:58.370
people who have been denied rights,

01:08:58.370 --> 01:09:00.550
people who have been denied opportunity,

01:09:00.550 --> 01:09:03.770
people who don't have the
same opportunity that I have

01:09:03.770 --> 01:09:06.357
and think, "Oh, okay,

01:09:06.357 --> 01:09:09.667
"I kinda wanna realign my
actions, the way I give

01:09:09.667 --> 01:09:11.647
"the way I spend my time
and the things I say

01:09:11.647 --> 01:09:13.267
"and who I vote for,"

01:09:17.537 --> 01:09:20.120
to take those people into account

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:22.500
rather than just think about
what I have and what's mine

01:09:22.500 --> 01:09:24.290
and what I can protect.

01:09:24.290 --> 01:09:26.303
And that may be an oversimplification,

01:09:28.910 --> 01:09:32.130
but it's been helpful for
me to see it that way.

01:09:32.130 --> 01:09:35.638
And I think it's a good
sort of North star as like,

01:09:35.638 --> 01:09:36.471
are you thinking about yourself

01:09:36.471 --> 01:09:37.480
or are you thinking about others?

01:09:37.480 --> 01:09:39.630
'Cause you only got one life to live

01:09:39.630 --> 01:09:42.130
unless we have more
that I don't know about.

01:09:42.130 --> 01:09:44.990
You won't know and if you do,

01:09:44.990 --> 01:09:46.810
you probably won't remember it

01:09:49.610 --> 01:09:52.560
unless you adhere to a
particular faith system

01:09:52.560 --> 01:09:57.530
that recognizes that and
then you do some sort of

01:09:57.530 --> 01:09:59.563
exploration of your past lives.

01:10:00.660 --> 01:10:03.320
But who's to say that
you'll be able to do that.

01:10:03.320 --> 01:10:06.360
You might find your self in
a worldview that denies that,

01:10:06.360 --> 01:10:07.940
and you'll miss out all the opportunities

01:10:07.940 --> 01:10:09.953
of living your subsequent lives.

